  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 253 PM EDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nThru Tonight...Low level trough axis over the adjacent Atlc waters\nis producing a north to northeast wind flow and steering scattered\nshowers and isolated storms toward the southwest. Saw an earlier\nstart to the convection today which could spell an early end this\nevening. However, reheating and lurking outflow boundaries will \nkeep scattered to numerous storms redeveloping. Wind gusts so far\nhave been tame in the storms today which is not surprising given \nthe limited DCAPE but gusts up to 40 mph cannot be ruled out in a\nstronger storms. The primary threat will continue to be locally \nheavy rain accumulating a quick 1-2 inches with locally up to 4 \ninches possible. \n\nAs the trough axis pushes westward across the area tonight, \nconvection should redevelop/focus over the Atlc with possible \nimpact to some coastal locations with areas north of the Cape \nappearing most likely. However, none of the CAM guidance is \nexplicitly showing convection pushing onshore overnight. \n\nWed-Thu...The trough axis will cross the area from east to west\nwhich will veer the wind flow out of the east Wed and southeast\nThu as the Bermuda high takes hold. Plume of deep moisture (PWATS\n2.2\"-2.4\") will remain over the area continuing the pattern of \nshowers and storms developing over the Atlantic waters in the \novernight/early morning, then shifting inland in the late morning \nand afternoon with the east coast sea breeze. Above normal \nafternoon rain chances (60-80%) are forecast and the saturated \natmosphere will continue to support locally high rainfall amounts \nof 2-3\" leading to minor flooding, and very low (up to 5%) chances\nfor +4\" of rainfall producing more impactful flooding. All of \nEast Central Florida remains outlined in a Marginal risk (level 1\nof 4) for excessive rainfall both days. Conditions are not\nfavorable for severe storms but wet downbursts may produce wind\ngusts up to 45 mph in the stronger storms. Temperatures remain \nseasonable in the upper 80s/lower 90s with peak afternoon heat \nindices 100-107. \n\nFri-Mon...Bermuda high ridge axis will remain across south or \ncentral FL resulting in light south to southwest flow with \nafternoon sea breezes along the coast. No significant drying is \nforecast as high moisture remains over the area keeping rain \nchances above normal. The highest chances generally inland sea \nbreeze collisions occur. Lower chances linger over the Atlantic \nwaters and along the coast into the overnights. Near normal \ntemperatures creep up a few degrees Friday and Saturday, with \nhighs mostly in the 90s but flirting with the M90s inland, \nbringing heat indices closer to 107. Highs then settle back to the\nupper 80s/lower 90s Sunday and Monday.",
